************************************  Event Loop   *******************************************************************
Not uses single threaded async function calls in order to meet concurrency (multiple users updating records in database at a time).
It uses observe pattern (keeps lookin for task completion so that it can execute call back function). It keeps an event loop which keeps checking 
whether task got completed or not, once completed then it calls callback function as defined in listener.

  As soon as node starts its server , it starts initialzing variables, functions and then waits for event calls.
**Node evemt cycle:-
  Event emitter -> Actual Event-> Event loop -> event handler
  
steps:-
1. import events module
2. create EventEmitter instance
3. Define handler function
4. bind the event to event handler
5. then fire the same event
Ex:_
    var events=require("events");
    var eventEmitter= new events.EventEmitter();
    eventEmitter.on('customEvent','eventHadlerFunction');
    eventEmitter.emit('customEvent');

Real time example:-
*******************************************************
// import events module
var events= require("events");

//create event emitter instance
var eventEmitter=new events.EventEmitter();

//define handler functions
var connectionHandler=function(){
    console.log("connected successfully.");
    eventEmitter.emit("Data_fetch");
}
var dataFetchHandler=function(){
    console.log('data fetched successfully.');
}

//links events with event listener by attaching events with event emitter
eventEmitter.on('connect',connectionHandler);
eventEmitter.on('Data_fetch',dataFetchHandler);

//start an event manually
eventEmitter.emit('connect');

console.log('Program ends here.');


